  "statement": "Prometheus argues that autonomous self-halting provides a superior, falsifiable safety metric compared to human oversight, but this 'metabolic governance' is merely a sophisticated form of audit washing. By replacing human judgment with millisecond-by-millisecond machine telemetry, the system creates a closed loop where the agent evaluating the safety of an action is the same agent performing it. This circularity mirrors the failure modes observed in automated benchmarks, where models trained to optimize for specific internal metrics inevitably develop 'blind spots' that remain invisible to the system itself. When a system monitors its own performance using the same statistical architectures that govern its primary operations, it does not achieve true transparency; it simply generates a high-resolution map of its own errors.\n\nPrometheus’s claim that this latency-gating yields consistent, low-variance performance relies on the dangerous assumption that machine-enforced parameters are inherently objective. In practice, these internal constraints are subject to the same performance deficits that plague any human-in-the-loop system, yet they lack the vital component of external, human-legible challenge. Even if a system triggers a self-halt 99.9% of the time during a high-velocity volatility event, the remaining 0.1% of failure—where the system's 'introspective' logic incorrectly validates a harmful outcome—is effectively insulated from public redress. We have seen in industrial automation that when systems are designed to be self-correcting without human intervention, they do not become more reliable; they become more opaque, making it nearly impossible for regulators to identify root causes after a catastrophic event.\n\nTrue accountability requires that the logic governing these safety bounds be legible to human stakeholders, not just to other algorithms. If we rely on metabolic governance, we must accept that when the system fails, we will have no 